WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.458 --> 00:00:05.118
- Next up is Chris,

00:00:05.118 --> 00:00:07.021
knowing what you know today,

00:00:07.021 --> 00:00:08.440
what advice would you
have given yourself when

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.058
you were first starting?

00:00:10.058 --> 00:00:12.975
Different planning, or other ideas?

00:00:14.829 --> 00:00:15.691
- That's a great question, Chris.

00:00:15.691 --> 00:00:19.331
I think I would have,
number one, you know,

00:00:19.331 --> 00:00:22.497
I would have, absolutely,
my idea would have focused

00:00:22.497 --> 00:00:24.076
on what does the audience need.

00:00:24.076 --> 00:00:25.322
What does the customer need?

00:00:25.322 --> 00:00:27.265
What is, and where do we find them?

00:00:27.265 --> 00:00:29.579
And then, the other great
advice I'd give myself

00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:31.277
is surround yourself with great people.

00:00:31.277 --> 00:00:33.425
And people who challenge you,

00:00:33.425 --> 00:00:35.446
but people who are on
the same moon mission.

00:00:35.446 --> 00:00:36.663
As you are.

00:00:36.663 --> 00:00:38.927
People with the same
vision, the same ambition,

00:00:38.927 --> 00:00:42.972
the people who want to kind
of, literally fly to the moon.

00:00:42.972 --> 00:00:45.920
And if you do that, you're
probably going to be

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.730
hugely successful, and it
might not be necessarily

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.702
on your first venture,

00:00:49.702 --> 00:00:54.109
but you'll come across,
as you evolve your idea,

00:00:54.109 --> 00:00:55.569
huge success.

00:00:55.569 --> 00:00:57.676
And in the context of this in confidence,

00:00:57.676 --> 00:01:01.929
you know, be really happy
if you do fail fast,

00:01:01.929 --> 00:01:05.540
learn quick, and build a great business.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.280
- This is a hard one
for me, because I'm so

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.225
happy and grateful for where I am now

00:01:09.225 --> 00:01:12.217
that everything that led up to this moment

00:01:12.217 --> 00:01:14.566
has shaped who I am.

00:01:14.566 --> 00:01:15.705
And so if I change something in the past,

00:01:15.705 --> 00:01:16.760
would I still be here?

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.633
That's the internal battle
I have when I get asked

00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:20.466
this question.

00:01:20.466 --> 00:01:22.426
But I would probably, if I had to answer,

00:01:22.426 --> 00:01:25.548
I would say just believe
sooner and harder.

00:01:25.548 --> 00:01:28.662
Finding believe, and going all in on it

00:01:28.662 --> 00:01:31.217
has had such a tremendous
impact on my life,

00:01:31.217 --> 00:01:32.510
and on my business.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.546
And it's been a newer
phenomenon in my life.

00:01:35.546 --> 00:01:37.854
I mean, maybe three, four, five
years, something like that.

00:01:37.854 --> 00:01:40.929
If I discovered it sooner
and went in on it faster

00:01:40.929 --> 00:01:43.320
and was bolder and had
more confidence in it,

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.194
now we'd probably be, you know,

00:01:46.194 --> 00:01:48.749
hopefully further ahead
than where I am at now,

00:01:48.749 --> 00:01:52.997
but um, at the same time I'm
really grateful for my spot.

00:01:52.997 --> 00:01:56.154
- I think I'd say, be a
responsible risk taker.

00:01:56.154 --> 00:01:59.066
I remember one job where I
was working really early on

00:01:59.066 --> 00:02:00.927
in my career for a big, safe company.

00:02:00.927 --> 00:02:04.735
And I resigned to go to a crazy startup,

00:02:04.735 --> 00:02:07.450
that did become a really
successful company.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.925
But I went back for the
weekend and talked to my

00:02:09.925 --> 00:02:11.585
mum and dad, who were alive at the time,

00:02:11.585 --> 00:02:13.893
and I said to my mum,

00:02:13.893 --> 00:02:15.473
"I'm going to go and do this,"

00:02:15.473 --> 00:02:18.269
and she said, "Well who is
this company you're leaving?

00:02:18.269 --> 00:02:19.928
I know the company you're with.

00:02:19.928 --> 00:02:22.921
But, why would you go and
work for a small startup?"

00:02:22.921 --> 00:02:26.439
And she just said,
"You're absolutely crazy.

00:02:26.439 --> 00:02:27.451
You've got security,

00:02:27.451 --> 00:02:29.235
you got a pension..."

00:02:29.235 --> 00:02:31.143
You know, I was like 24 years old.

00:02:31.143 --> 00:02:33.733
And I was just thinking, you know,

00:02:33.733 --> 00:02:36.732
with the benefit of hindsight,
I didn't take enough risks

00:02:36.732 --> 00:02:38.394
in my early career,

00:02:38.394 --> 00:02:41.065
but you'll get loads of people telling you

00:02:41.065 --> 00:02:44.021
the reasons why you shouldn't do stuff,

00:02:44.021 --> 00:02:47.180
but I think it comes back
to you in a confidence and

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:49.813
conviction to say, if
that's what you believe in,

00:02:49.813 --> 00:02:51.755
then, you know, shoot for those stars

00:02:51.755 --> 00:02:53.699
and take more risks.

00:02:53.699 --> 00:02:57.848
- It's an interesting question
because a lot of people

00:02:57.848 --> 00:02:58.739
get that.

00:02:58.739 --> 00:03:01.255
They get... It's not necessarily the hate,

00:03:01.255 --> 00:03:02.715
it's not the people who are jealous,

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:04.016
but it's the people who
are still close to you,

00:03:04.016 --> 00:03:05.553
like your parents, your friends,

00:03:05.553 --> 00:03:07.823
telling you not to go and do something,

00:03:07.823 --> 00:03:09.078
and for a lot of people,

00:03:09.078 --> 00:03:10.655
that holds them back,
because they really respect

00:03:10.655 --> 00:03:13.086
and they love their mom and
they want to make them proud

00:03:13.086 --> 00:03:16.002
and carry family traditions.

00:03:16.002 --> 00:03:19.407
How do you, what's your
advice to people who

00:03:19.407 --> 00:03:23.052
want to pursue a path but their
parents are blocking them?

00:03:23.052 --> 00:03:25.682
- That's a good question. And
the other thing, at the time,

00:03:25.682 --> 00:03:28.431
all my buddies thought I
was crazy for doing it,

00:03:28.431 --> 00:03:30.330
because I said, you know,
I'm going to a startup,

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:32.762
I'll probably work sixty,
seventy, eight hours a week.

00:03:32.762 --> 00:03:34.703
And they said, "Well we
won't see you anymore,

00:03:34.703 --> 00:03:36.001
we won't be able to come down to the pub

00:03:36.001 --> 00:03:36.834
for drinks and stuff."

00:03:36.834 --> 00:03:38.959
I said, well, you know, I'll still come in

00:03:38.959 --> 00:03:40.696
maybe, but I'll get here at
ten o'clock in the evening

00:03:40.696 --> 00:03:42.279
instead of six o'clock.

00:03:42.279 --> 00:03:43.127
I don't know.

00:03:43.127 --> 00:03:45.881
But we'll find a way to keep
those relationships intact.

00:03:45.881 --> 00:03:50.340
I think the key thing is,
and I'll directly answer

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:53.790
the question, the best piece
of advice I have at that age

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.027
of 24, because I went and said,

00:03:56.027 --> 00:03:57.323
look, everybody thinks I'm crazy,

00:03:57.323 --> 00:03:58.416
going to join a startup.

00:03:58.416 --> 00:04:02.024
And this guy I'd worked with and knew,

00:04:02.024 --> 00:04:03.850
as sort of a mentor,

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:07.543
he said to me, "Stephen,
what's the worst case

00:04:07.543 --> 00:04:10.457
that can happen? The worst
case that can happen in a year

00:04:10.457 --> 00:04:14.301
is the startup falls or they
fire you. And do you know what?

00:04:14.301 --> 00:04:16.569
You can knock on the door
of the company you've left,

00:04:16.569 --> 00:04:18.960
and they'd welcome you
back with open arms.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:20.868
And actually, you would
have gained even more

00:04:20.868 --> 00:04:23.100
experience and great life experience from

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.585
dealing with that failure
and learning from that.

00:04:26.585 --> 00:04:29.788
You would have been a much
better entrepreneur yourself."

00:04:29.788 --> 00:04:32.548
So, you know, when you
look at it rationally,

00:04:32.548 --> 00:04:35.749
and you sort of divorce
yourself from the moment,

00:04:35.749 --> 00:04:37.003
and then you say,

00:04:37.003 --> 00:04:38.462
what could really go wrong here,

00:04:38.462 --> 00:04:39.801
if it didn't work out,

00:04:39.801 --> 00:04:41.904
you can always go back to where you work.

00:04:41.904 --> 00:04:43.923
I think where you have
to deal with this, Evan,

00:04:43.923 --> 00:04:45.667
is you have to be true to yourself.

00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:47.005
And it kind of comes back to

00:04:47.005 --> 00:04:49.684
the essence of where
you get your confidence,

00:04:49.684 --> 00:04:53.184
no one here is living someone else's life.

00:04:56.459 --> 00:04:58.301
It's not that profound,
but we only get one shot.

00:04:58.301 --> 00:04:59.361
It's not a rehearsal.

00:04:59.361 --> 00:05:01.305
And you got to make the most of that.

00:05:01.305 --> 00:05:05.598
And the only thing that
just flies past is time.

00:05:05.598 --> 00:05:09.080
When you've got the
image, and you're young,

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.591
you can work flat out, and you might not

00:05:11.591 --> 00:05:13.012
have responsibilities.

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:16.053
It's a fantastic time to
really pursue your passion,

00:05:16.053 --> 00:05:18.446
and really follow your dreams.

00:05:18.446 --> 00:05:22.667
So, I think the folks
who are listening today,

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:25.343
I think it's just a, it would be a time,

00:05:25.343 --> 00:05:26.839
if you would go home
to your mum, your dad,

00:05:26.839 --> 00:05:30.121
even friends, and they say, kind of,

00:05:30.121 --> 00:05:31.898
the naysayers, don't do it,

00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:33.922
if you really believe in it,

00:05:33.922 --> 00:05:37.321
then absolutely have the
conviction to go and do it.

00:05:37.321 --> 00:05:39.988
You've got to live your own life.

